   "Consult",
   "Neurology",
   "M. Castellanos, MD",
   "Referred by Dr. Haun for proximal leg weakness. 71M with newly dx RUL LCNEC. ~2-3 mo of difficulty rising from chairs, climbing stairs, getting out of tub; arms less so. Dry mouth (on LAMA but predates?), constipation, ED, mild intermittent ptosis evenings. Exam: no fasciculations; hip flexors 4/5, deltoids 4+/5. Reflexes absent at knees/ankles at rest; patellar reflex elicited after 10 s maximal quadriceps contraction. Hip flexor strength improves on repeated testing over ~10 s. Mild bilateral ptosis, no fatigable ptosis on sustained upgaze. Sensation intact. Assessment: proximal weakness with areflexia, post-exercise facilitation and autonomic symptoms in setting of neuroendocrine lung carcinoma - highly suggestive of Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome. DDx: myasthenia gravis, paraneoplastic myopathy, steroid-free so not steroid myopathy. Plan: P/Q-type VGCC Ab, AChR Ab, paraneoplastic panel, CK, TSH, B12. EMG/NCS with repetitive stimulation and post-exercise CMAP - first available 11/04/2026. Will discuss amifampridine if confirmed. Note to surgical team: patients with LEMS have increased sensitivity to neuromuscular blocking drugs - recommend anesthesia be made aware. cc: Dr. Haun."
